{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be helping take care of you tonight. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been having terrible abdominal pain, especially after eating. And I feel that familiar tightness in my chest again—like my mast cells are flaring up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, I see you have a history of mast cell degranulation syndrome and esophageal spasms. Did anything trigger this episode?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Not sure. I didn’t eat anything unusual. But I’ve had this feeling before—itching, flushing, trouble breathing. I knew I’d need Benadryl soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your vitals show your blood pressure is a bit low—87 over 51. We’ll keep an eye on that. You’re also wheezing a little. We’ve started your standard treatment: IV steroids, Benadryl, and some Dilaudid for pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. That protocol usually helps. But the pain is really sharp—right here in the upper belly. Feels like my esophagus is cramping again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your CT scan of the abdomen came back normal—no gallstones, no appendicitis. So it’s likely related to your known esophageal dysmotility, possibly worsened by the mast cell flare."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I haven’t had a bowel movement in a few days either. That’s not helping. And my face feels swollen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That facial swelling and the rash on your chest are common during mast cell activation. We’ll continue monitoring. We’ve given you fluids and will repeat your labs in the morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. Should I stop my blood pressure meds? I’m on carvedilol and furosemide at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve held both for now because your blood pressure has been on the lower side. Once the crisis settles, your regular doctors can reassess if you still need them."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. I just don’t want my heart to suffer because we’re focusing on the mast cells."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2193-04-04 18:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good point. Your EKG and three troponin levels are normal, so no sign of heart damage. Your coronary disease is stable. We’re just being cautious with the meds during this episode."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2193-04-05 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs look better. White count is down, and liver enzymes are improving. We’re planning an EGD today to check your esophagus and possibly inject Botox to help with the spasms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2193-04-05 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had that before. It helps for a little while, but the spasms always come back. Do you think it’ll make a difference this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2193-04-05 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It may give temporary relief. The goal is to reduce the frequency of spasms, especially since they seem tied to your mast cell symptoms. We’ll do it under sedation, and you’ll be monitored closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2193-04-05 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust the team. Just please avoid any contrast or dyes—they’ve triggered reactions before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2193-04-05 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. We’ve noted your allergies to iodine, barium, and contrast. No dyes will be used. The procedure is just the scope and Botox injection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, you’re back from the EGD. The procedure went well. They injected Botox at the gastroesophageal junction. No signs of ulcers or strictures."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Did it help with the pain? I still feel tightness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It might take a day or two to see improvement. You did have some relief briefly, but the mast cell symptoms flared again. We gave you another dose of Benadryl and Dilaudid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m so tired of this cycle. It feels like one problem feeds the other."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. Your allergist and GI team agree they’re linked. That’s why we’re treating both—calming the mast cells helps the spasms, and relaxing the esophagus may reduce triggers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my nausea? I haven’t eaten much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2193-04-05 13:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve added Zofran for nausea. You can try small sips of clear liquids. If you tolerate that, we’ll advance your diet slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood pressure is more stable—123 over 72. You’re not wheezing as much, and your rash is fading."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do feel a bit better. Still some chest tightness, but not as intense. Can I go home soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow. We want to make sure you’re stable off IV meds. You’ve switched back to oral Dilaudid and Benadryl, which you’ll continue at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need to take Benadryl that often forever?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your allergist will help manage that. For now, we’re giving you diphenhydramine tablets to take by mouth every four hours as needed for tightness or breathing issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my constipation? I still haven’t gone."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve added bisacodyl and senna to help. Stay on the polyethylene glycol too. Hydration and fiber will help once you’re home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ll follow up with my GI and allergist. They’re both scheduled next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. We’ll send them a summary. In the meantime, keep taking your omeprazole, methadone, and gabapentin as prescribed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my heart meds? Should I restart them?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2193-04-06 08:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hold off for now. Your blood pressure is still on the lower side. Your cardiologist should reassess once you’re fully recovered. We’ve noted that in your discharge instructions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well overnight—no episodes of low blood pressure or breathing trouble. We’re clearing you for discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. It’s a relief to be going home. Just want to feel normal again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve sent prescriptions for diphenhydramine, senna, and bisacodyl. Continue all your other meds except carvedilol and furosemide for now. Call your doctors if symptoms return."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And if I feel a flare coming on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take your Benadryl, use your albuterol inhaler, and call your allergist. If you can’t reach them or feel worse, go to the ER. You know your body best."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do. Thanks for listening and for not rushing me. I know I’ve been here a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re not a burden. We’re here to help you through these flares. Take care, and we hope to see you only in clinic."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2193-04-07 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do my best. Thank you, Doctor."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2193-04-04 17:16:00",
    "admission_end": "2193-04-07 13:50:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 60-year-old woman with a history of mast cell degranulation syndrome, achalasia, and coronary artery disease s/p CABG was admitted for abdominal pain and a self-reported mast cell crisis. She presented with epigastric pain worsened by eating, chest tightness, flushing, and wheezing. Treatment followed her allergist’s protocol with IV methylprednisolone, Benadryl, and IV Dilaudid for pain. A CT abdomen showed no acute findings, and troponins remained negative, ruling out cardiac ischemia. She underwent an EGD with Botox injection at the gastroesophageal junction, which provided only transient relief. Her symptoms improved over three days with supportive care. Blood pressure-lowering medications were held due to hypotension during the crisis. She was discharged home with instructions to follow up with her allergist and GI specialist, continue oral symptom management, and restart chronic medications as advised by her providers.",
    "problems": [
      "Congenital cutaneous mastocytosis",
      "Chronic diastolic (congestive) heart failure",
      "Hypotension, unspecified",
      "Achalasia of cardia",
      "Major depressive disorder, single episode, unspecified",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Epigastric pain",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension"
    ]
  }
}
